::Q01::Which method reads the entire contents of a file as a string?{
~readline()
~readlines()
=read()
~tell()
}

::Q02::[markdown]What does `read(10)` do when used on a file opened in read mode?{
=Reads the first 10 characters from the current position
~Reads the first 10 lines of the file
~Returns the file contents as a list
~Moves the cursor 10 positions without reading
}

::Q03::Which method returns the file's lines as a list, with each line represented as an element?{
~read()
~readline()
=readlines()
~tell()
}

::Q04::[markdown]What does the `tell()` method return when used with a file object?{
~The number of lines remaining in the file
~The file's extension
=The current cursor position in the file
~The total size of the file
}

::Q05::[markdown]Which statement correctly describes binary mode when reading files?{
~Binary mode prevents the use of `read()` and `readline()`
=Binary mode is required for read/write operations on non-text-based files
~Binary mode automatically converts binary data into text
~Binary mode is required only for CSV files
}
